insert into concept (slug, name, area_slug, kind, language, framework, brief, lesson, content_status) values
  ('bytes-vs-characters', 'Bytes vs characters', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A byte is a unit of storage. A character is a unit of text. In UTF-8 one character takes one to four bytes.', null, 'reviewed'),
  ('explicit-charsets', 'Explicit charsets', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Decoding bytes needs a named encoding. Relying on a platform default breaks when the tool runs elsewhere.', '{"how_it_works":"Decoding needs an encoding. If code does not name one, the language picks a default, and defaults differ: the\noperating system''s setting, the user''s locale, or a fixed value that changed between versions. Java used the\nplatform encoding until Java 18, so the same program decoded files as Windows-1252 on many Windows machines and\nas UTF-8 on Linux. Since Java 18 the default is UTF-8, but older code and other languages still vary.\n\nThe safe habit is to name the encoding every time bytes become text or text becomes bytes, usually UTF-8. Then\nthe program gives the same answer on a laptop, a server and a CI runner.\n\nSometimes the encoding has to be discovered. Protocols state it: HTTP in the Content-Type header, XML in its\ndeclaration, email in its headers. Plain files usually say nothing. A byte order mark at the start is a strong\nhint: EF BB BF for UTF-8, FF FE or FE FF for UTF-16. Without one a program can only guess, which is why formats\nsuch as JSON simply require UTF-8.\n\nWhen a UTF-8 file does start with a byte order mark, decide what to do with it. Most readers should skip it,\nsince it is not part of the text.","examples":["A CSV saved by Excel as CSV UTF-8 starts with EF BB BF. A script that does not skip it reads the first column name as an invisible character followed by name, and lookups by that name fail.","A program that shows é correctly on a developer''s Mac shows Ã© on a Windows build server, because it decoded with the default encoding instead of UTF-8.","A web page served with the wrong charset in its Content-Type header shows mojibake, although the file itself is fine."],"misconceptions":["A text file records its own encoding. Most do not, so the reader must know or guess.","The default encoding is always UTF-8. It depends on the language, its version and the machine.","A byte order mark is harmless. Many tools treat it as part of the first line."],"elsewhere":["Database connections have their own encoding, and a mismatch corrupts names on the way in.","Command-line tools read the locale to decide how to decode, so the same command can count differently on two machines.","APIs that accept text should say which encoding they expect, and reject or convert anything else."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('decoding-across-chunks', 'Decoding across chunk boundaries', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A chunk of bytes can end in the middle of a character, so a decoder keeps the leftover bytes for the next chunk.', '{"how_it_works":"A streaming program reads bytes in chunks, and chunk boundaries fall wherever the buffer happens to fill. In\nUTF-8 a character can take up to four bytes, so a chunk can end after the first byte of é or after two bytes of\nan emoji.\n\nIf each chunk is decoded on its own, the decoder sees an unfinished sequence at the end of one chunk and stray\ncontinuation bytes at the start of the next. It reports both as invalid, usually replacing each with the\nreplacement character U+FFFD, so one real character becomes two wrong ones. The bug depends on where the boundary\nfalls: the same file can count correctly with one buffer size and wrongly with another, and small test files that\nfit in one chunk never show it.\n\nThe fix is a decoder that keeps state between chunks. It decodes every complete character in a chunk and holds\nback the bytes of an unfinished one, then puts them in front of the next chunk. At the very end of the input, any\nbytes still held back are a truncated character, which is invalid. Languages provide such decoders, as a reader\nthat wraps a byte stream or as a decoder object that accepts partial input.\n\nByte-level counts such as bytes and newlines need no decoding at all, because in UTF-8 a newline byte never\nappears inside a multi-byte character.","examples":["A tool counts characters correctly on a short test file but reports a few extra on a large Chinese text, where three-byte characters often straddle the 4096-byte boundary.","A chat server that decodes each network packet separately sometimes shows a broken symbol in the middle of a message with emoji, whenever a packet boundary splits one.","A log shipper that cuts files into fixed-size pieces for upload must cut on line boundaries, or each piece can no longer be decoded on its own."],"misconceptions":["Decoding chunk by chunk is the same as decoding the whole file. Only when no character crosses a boundary.","A bigger buffer fixes it. It only makes boundaries rarer, so the bug hides better.","Counting bytes and lines needs decoding too. In UTF-8 newline bytes can be counted on the raw bytes."],"elsewhere":["Anything that carries text over a stream, such as chat, WebSockets or server-sent events, needs a decoder that keeps state.","Compression and encryption streams have the same shape: state is carried from one block to the next.","Editors that open huge files in pieces must align the pieces to character boundaries."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('streaming-constant-memory', 'Streaming in constant memory', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'Read input in fixed-size chunks and keep running totals, so memory does not grow with the input.', '{"how_it_works":"A program that reads a whole file into memory before working on it needs as much memory as the file is big.\nThat is fine for a 2 KB file and fatal for a 20 GB log on a machine with 8 GB of memory: the program slows to a\ncrawl as the system swaps, or the operating system stops it.\n\nA streaming program keeps a fixed-size buffer, perhaps 64 KB, fills it with the next part of the input, updates\na few running totals and reuses the same buffer for the next part. Its memory depends on the buffer and the\ntotals, not on the input, so it is the same for 2 KB and for 20 GB. The time still grows with the input, since\nevery byte is read once, but the memory does not.\n\nNot every question can be answered this way. Counting bytes, lines and words, finding the longest line or adding\nup a column needs only a few numbers carried from one chunk to the next. Counting distinct words, sorting lines or\nfinding the median needs memory that grows with the input, because the answer depends on everything seen so far.\nThe skill is to ask, before writing code, what state the answer has to carry.\n\nStreaming also lets a program produce output before its input has finished arriving, which is what makes pipes\nbetween tools work.","examples":["Counting the lines of a 20 GB web server log with a streaming counter takes a few megabytes of memory and finishes as fast as the disk can be read. Loading it first fails on most laptops.","A video player decodes a few seconds ahead and throws away what it has shown, so a two-hour film never sits in memory at once.","Database backups are written and read as streams, so the size of the database is not limited by the memory of the machine that makes the backup."],"misconceptions":["Reading line by line is always streaming. It is, until the code collects the lines into a list.","A bigger buffer is always faster. Beyond some tens of kilobytes the disk is the limit, and more buffer only costs memory.","Streaming is only for huge files. The same code handles small files and pipes, so there is no reason to write two versions."],"elsewhere":["HTTP servers stream request and response bodies, so a large upload does not need to fit in memory.","grep, gzip, checksum programs and the merge phase of sort all stream.","Data pipelines are designed around what state they must keep per record."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('stdin-vs-file', 'Standard input vs file', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A file and standard input can be treated as the same kind of byte stream, so one loop serves both.', '{"how_it_works":"Unix tools are built to be joined. A tool given a file name opens and reads that file; a tool given no name reads\nstandard input, which is whatever the shell connects to it: the keyboard, a file redirected into it, or the output\nof another program through a pipe. Many tools also accept a single dash as a name that means standard input.\n\nA file and standard input are both byte streams, read in the same way under the same read contract. So a tool\nshould open the right source and hand it to one shared counting loop, not keep one code path for files and\nanother for pipes.\n\nSome things are true only of files. A file has a size that can be asked for without reading it, and a position\nthat can be moved back. A pipe has neither: its size is unknown until the writer finishes, and data once read is\ngone. A tool that takes a shortcut that only works on files, such as asking for the size instead of counting\nbytes, breaks the moment it is used in a pipeline.\n\nThe output side mirrors this: a tool writes its results to standard output, so the next tool in a pipeline, or a\nfile, can receive them.","examples":["Piping a compressed log through a decompressor into wc counts its lines without ever writing the 20 GB of uncompressed text to disk.","wc prints the file name next to its counts, but reading a pipe there is no name to print, so it prints the counts alone.","A program that rewinds its input to read it twice works on files and fails, or hangs, on a pipe."],"misconceptions":["Standard input is the keyboard. It is whatever the shell connects to the program.","A pipe is a temporary file. It is a buffer in the operating system, with no size and no rewind.","Supporting pipes needs a separate code path. One loop over a byte stream serves both."],"elsewhere":["Compilers, formatters and build tools read standard input so editors and scripts can pipe code through them.","Containers and services write logs to standard output so the platform can collect the stream.","Network sockets behave like pipes: no size and no rewind."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('stream-read-contract', 'The read contract', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A read can return fewer bytes than the buffer holds. The returned count says how many are valid, and -1 means the end.', '{"how_it_works":"When a program asks the operating system to read into a buffer, it says how many bytes it wants at most. The\nread returns how many it actually delivered, which can be anything from one to the size of the buffer. Only\nthose bytes are valid. The rest of the buffer still holds whatever was there before, often bytes from the\nprevious read.\n\nA short read does not mean the input has ended. A file usually fills the buffer until its last part, but a pipe\ndelivers whatever the writing program has produced so far, and a network socket whatever packets have arrived.\nOnly a special result says the end has been reached: zero bytes in C and most operating systems, or minus one\nfrom an InputStream in Java.\n\nSo a correct read loop follows three rules. Use the returned count, never the buffer size, for everything done\nwith the data. Keep reading after a short read. Stop only on the end marker. A loop that breaks the first rule\ncounts leftover bytes again. A loop that breaks the second stops early on pipes and networks while appearing to\nwork on files, which is why the bug often survives testing.","examples":["A 10 KB file read with a 4 KB buffer usually comes back as 4 KB, 4 KB and 2 KB, then the end marker. A program that always adds 4 KB reports 12 KB.","When a slow program writes into a pipe a line at a time, each read may return one short line, although much more is coming.","Network code that expects one read to return one whole message works on a fast local network and fails when a message is split across packets."],"misconceptions":["A read fills the buffer unless the input is over. It can return fewer bytes at any time.","Getting fewer bytes than asked for means the end. Only the end marker means the end.","The bytes beyond the count are zero. They are left over from earlier reads, so counting them adds real-looking data."],"elsewhere":["Sockets follow the same contract, which is why network protocols need message framing.","Every language wraps it, for example InputStream in Java, the read call in C and streams in Node.","Writes have a matching contract: a write can accept fewer bytes than offered, and the rest must be written again."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('line-endings', 'Lines and line endings', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A line is the text up to and including a newline byte, so tools count lines by counting newlines. A last line with no newline after it is not counted. Files written on Windows end each line with a carriage return and a newline, and the carriage return is an extra byte that line counting ignores but byte counting sees.', '{"how_it_works":"A file has no lines, only bytes. A line is a convention: in Unix, the text up to a newline byte, 0A. POSIX\ndefines a line as characters followed by a newline, so a file whose last line has no newline ends with an\nincomplete line. wc counts newline bytes, so for such a file it reports one line fewer than an editor shows.\n\nWindows ends each line with two bytes, a carriage return 0D and then a newline 0A, a habit from typewriters and\nteleprinters, where returning the carriage and feeding the paper were separate actions. Classic Mac systems used\na carriage return alone. Most modern tools accept both, but not all.\n\nThe carriage return causes quiet bugs. Counting lines by newline still works, but the carriage return stays at\nthe end of each line''s text, so a comparison with yes fails because the value is really yes followed by an\ninvisible character. It is also a byte, and whitespace for word counting. Git can convert line endings on\ncheckout, which changes a file''s byte count between Windows and Linux machines.\n\nSplitting a whole text at its newlines also produces an empty last piece when the file ends with a newline, and\ncode must not treat that piece as a real line.","examples":["A CSV exported on Windows and read on Linux keeps a carriage return at the end of every last column, so a filter for the value active matches nothing.","An editor shows 10 lines and wc -l reports 9, because the last line has no newline. Each is right by its own definition.","A test that checks the byte count of a fixture passes on Linux and fails on a Windows CI runner, because Git converted the line endings on checkout."],"misconceptions":["A line ends however the operating system ends it. Files move between systems, so a tool must handle both endings.","The number of lines is the number of newlines plus one. Not for a file that ends with a newline, and not for an empty one.","The carriage return is harmless because it is invisible. It changes comparisons, lengths and byte counts."],"elsewhere":["HTTP, SMTP and many other protocols require a carriage return and a newline at the end of every header line.","Git''s autocrlf setting and attributes file exist to control this conversion.","Diff tools show a whole file as changed when only its line endings changed."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('word-state-machine', 'Counting words with a state machine', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A word counter walks the text once and remembers one thing: whether it is inside a word. A word starts when a non-space character follows a space or the start of input, so runs of spaces never make empty words. The remembered state has to survive from one chunk of input to the next.', '{"how_it_works":"What is a word? wc''s answer is simple and strict: a word is a run of characters that are not whitespace.\nPunctuation is part of the word, so don''t, e-mail and 3.14 are each one word, and a dash with spaces around it is\na word of its own. Spell checkers and search engines use the more complex word boundary rules of Unicode; wc does\nnot.\n\nThat definition fits a tiny state machine. The counter remembers one thing: whether it is inside a word. Reading\na non-space while outside a word starts a word, so the count goes up and the state becomes inside. Reading\nwhitespace makes the state outside. Runs of spaces therefore never create empty words, and leading or trailing\nspaces do not matter.\n\nThe state has to survive chunk boundaries. If a word straddles two chunks and the counter forgets that it was\ninside a word, the second half counts as a new word. Carrying one flag from one chunk to the next fixes it, and\nthat flag is the whole state.\n\nThe hard part is deciding what whitespace is. Space, tab, newline, carriage return, form feed and vertical tab\nare the classic set. Unicode adds others, such as the no-break space U+00A0, the em space and the ideographic\nspace used in Chinese and Japanese text. Which of them count depends on the tool, its version and the locale, so\nthe same file can have different word counts on two machines.","examples":["The phrase rock-and-roll is one word to wc and three to a person or a search engine.","Text pasted from a web page often has no-break spaces between words, and a counter that does not treat them as whitespace reports fewer words than the author expects.","Japanese text usually has no spaces between words, so wc counts whole sentences as single words."],"misconceptions":["Counting words means counting spaces and adding one. Double spaces, leading spaces and empty files break that.","A word is what a dictionary calls a word. For wc it is any run of non-whitespace.","Whitespace means the space bar. Tabs, newlines and several Unicode characters count too, depending on the locale."],"elsewhere":["Lexers in compilers use the same idea with more states: inside a number, a string or a comment.","Editors and search engines use the Unicode word boundary rules to select and index words.","Protocol parsers carry a small state across network packets in exactly this way."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('differential-testing', 'Testing against a reference tool', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Run your tool and a trusted reference on the same inputs and compare the outputs. Your own expected values only test what you already believe, while the reference exposes cases you did not think of, such as odd whitespace or line endings. Pin the reference version and settings, since they can change its answers.', '{"how_it_works":"Unit tests compare your program with expected values that you wrote. That checks the cases you thought of,\nagainst your understanding of the right answer. If your understanding is wrong, the code and the test are wrong\ntogether, and the test passes.\n\nDifferential testing compares your program with another implementation on the same input, often a trusted\nreference tool. When the outputs differ, one of the two is wrong, and finding out which teaches you something you\ndid not know, such as how the reference treats a no-break space or a file without a final newline.\n\nIt works best with many and varied inputs: an empty file, one without a final newline, Windows line endings,\naccented text, emoji, invalid bytes, a file larger than the buffer, and piped input as well as named files.\nGenerating random inputs and comparing automatically, a form of fuzzing, finds cases nobody would write by hand.\n\nThe reference must be pinned. Different versions and builds of wc disagree on some inputs, and the locale changes\nthe answers, so record which version and settings produced the expected output.","examples":["The real wc in Git Bash reports 58070 words for the challenge''s file when run from a script and 58164 in its terminal, because the locale differs, so a test that compares against it must pin the locale first.","Compiler teams compile the same programs with two compilers and compare the results to find miscompilations.","When a service is rewritten, running the old and new versions side by side on real traffic and comparing their responses catches differences before users do."],"misconceptions":["If my unit tests pass, my tool matches the original. Only for the cases and answers you wrote down.","The reference is always right. It has bugs and version differences too, so a difference is a question, not a verdict.","Comparing a couple of files is enough. The bugs live in the unusual inputs."],"elsewhere":["Browser engines, databases and JSON parsers are tested against each other and against shared test suites.","Large refactorings often rely on comparing old and new outputs.","Data migrations compare row counts and checksums between the old store and the new one."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('site-directory-layout', 'Directory layout as the data model', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A site generator treats folders as its data model. Content holds what the author writes, themes hold how pages look, and the output folder holds what gets published. Keeping them apart lets each change without touching the others, and lets the output be rebuilt from the other two.', null, 'generated'),
  ('cli-subcommands', 'Command-line arguments and exit codes', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A command-line tool reads its options, subcommands and file names from its arguments, and rejects ones it does not understand. It writes results to standard output and problems to the error stream, and exits with a non-zero status on failure, so scripts can tell success from failure.', '{"how_it_works":"A command-line tool has a contract with its caller, which is often another program rather than a person. The\ncontract has three channels.\n\nStandard output carries the result and nothing else, so it can be piped into the next tool or saved to a file.\nStandard error carries messages for the person: warnings, usage help and errors. Keeping the two apart means an\nerror message never ends up inside a file of results.\n\nThe exit status, a small number returned when the program ends, says whether it worked. Zero means success and\nanything else means failure, and some tools use different values for different problems. Shells, scripts and CI\nsystems check it, so a step that fails but exits with zero lets a broken build pass.\n\nArguments complete the contract. Options such as -c and -l choose what to do and can usually be combined, the\nremaining words are file names, and an unknown option is an error with a usage message, not something to ignore.\nWhen several files are given and one cannot be read, a well-behaved tool reports that file on standard error,\nstill processes the others, and exits with a non-zero status at the end.","examples":["A deploy script runs the tests and checks their exit status. If the tests fail but the command exits with zero, broken code ships.","A tool that prints its errors on standard output corrupts every report that saves its output to a file.","GNU wc given three files, one of them missing, prints counts for the two that exist and a total, writes an error about the third on standard error, and exits with status 1."],"misconceptions":["Exit codes are for people to read. They are mainly for other programs.","Printing an error is enough. Without a non-zero exit status a script cannot tell that anything went wrong.","Results and error messages can share one stream. Mixing them breaks pipelines."],"elsewhere":["CI systems mark a step as failed purely from its exit status.","HTTP status codes play the same role for web APIs that exit statuses play for commands.","Container platforms decide whether to restart a service from how its process exited."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('untrusted-paths', 'Untrusted names in file paths', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A name that ends up inside a file path, from a command line or a web request, can contain .. and climb out of the folder you meant. Resolve the full path, normalise it, and check that it still sits inside the base folder before you read or write.', null, 'generated'),
  ('markup-to-html', 'Converting Markdown to HTML', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A Markdown renderer works in two layers. It finds the block structure first, such as headings and paragraphs, then handles inline marks such as emphasis and links inside each block. The result is written out as HTML elements.', null, 'generated'),
  ('output-escaping', 'Escaping text inserted into HTML', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Text placed into HTML must be escaped, so that characters such as <, > and & show as text and are not read as markup. Escape plain values at the point they are inserted, and leave alone text that is already HTML.', null, 'generated'),
  ('template-substitution', 'Template substitution', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A template is text with named holes, such as {{ Title }}. Filling it in one pass over the template, instead of one replace per name, keeps text that merely looks like a placeholder inside a value from being filled in a second time.', null, 'generated'),
  ('build-pipeline', 'Build pipeline', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A build reads inputs, transforms them and writes outputs, and the same inputs should always give the same output. Writing everything into a freshly cleared output folder, instead of editing files in place, keeps stale files out and makes the result safe to publish as a whole.', null, 'generated'),
  ('static-file-serving', 'Serving static files over HTTP', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A static server turns a URL path into a file. It maps / to index.html, sends the file with the right Content-Type, and answers 404 when nothing matches. The browser trusts the Content-Type header, not the file extension.', null, 'generated'),
  ('file-watching', 'Watching files for changes', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A tool can notice file changes by polling modification times on a timer, or by asking the operating system for change notifications. Notifications are faster and cheaper but platform-specific, and they often arrive in bursts, so a tool waits briefly (debouncing) before it acts.', null, 'generated'),
  ('live-reload-push', 'Pushing a reload to the browser', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A page cannot know that the server rebuilt it unless something tells it. The development server keeps a connection open, for example with server-sent events, and sends a message after each rebuild. A small script added to pages in development only then reloads the browser.', null, 'generated'),
  ('delimited-fields', 'Fields in delimited text', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Tab- and comma-separated files store one record per line, split into fields at a delimiter character. Fields are numbered from 1, and two delimiters in a row mean an empty field, not a missing one. Plain splitting breaks when a value contains the delimiter inside quotes, which full CSV parsers handle and simple tools do not.', null, 'generated'),
  ('unix-pipelines', 'Composing tools with pipes', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'Small tools that read standard input and write standard output can be chained, so each does one job and the shell joins them. Lines of text are the shared format. The order of the chain matters, because some tools only work on what they see next to each other, such as uniq, which only removes adjacent duplicates.', null, 'generated'),
  ('regex-matching', 'Matching with regular expressions', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A regular expression describes a set of strings using literal characters, classes such as any digit or any word character, and anchors for the start and end of a line. A line matches when the pattern succeeds starting at some position in it, so an unanchored pattern can match anywhere and an empty pattern matches every line.', null, 'generated'),
  ('recursive-file-walk', 'Walking a directory tree', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'To visit every file under a folder, list its entries, handle the files, and descend into each subfolder the same way. The order entries come back in is not guaranteed, symbolic links can form loops, and some folders cannot be read, so a walker needs a rule for each.', null, 'generated'),
  ('case-folding', 'Comparing text without case', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A case-insensitive match maps both the pattern and the text to one common form before comparing. In ASCII that is a simple swap between upper and lower letters, but in Unicode some letters change length or have more than one lower-case form, so the language''s case-folding rules are safer than a hand-made table.', null, 'generated'),
  ('lexical-analysis', 'Lexing text into tokens', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A lexer reads characters and groups them into tokens, such as a brace, a string, a number or a keyword, and skips the whitespace between them. The parser then works with meaningful pieces instead of single characters, and the lexer is the natural place to reject a character that cannot start any token.', null, 'generated'),
  ('recursive-descent-parsing', 'Recursive descent parsing', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A recursive descent parser has one function per rule of the grammar, and a rule that contains other rules calls their functions, so nesting in the input becomes nesting of calls. It looks at the next token to decide which rule applies and reports an error at the first token no rule allows.', null, 'generated'),
  ('json-grammar', 'The JSON grammar', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A JSON value is an object, an array, a string, a number, true, false or null, and objects and arrays can hold any value, nested as deep as you like. The rules are strict: keys are double-quoted strings, there are no trailing commas or comments, and numbers have no leading zeros.', null, 'generated'),
  ('conformance-test-suite', 'Conformance test suites', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A conformance suite is a shared set of inputs, each with the verdict a correct implementation must give, valid or invalid. Running it shows whether you follow the specification in the corners nobody thinks of, and an invalid input that is accepted is as much a failure as a valid one that is rejected.', null, 'generated'),
  ('frequency-map', 'Counting with a map', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'To count how often each symbol occurs, keep a map from symbol to count and add one for every symbol you read. One pass over the input is enough, and the map grows with the number of distinct symbols, not with the size of the input.', null, 'generated'),
  ('priority-queue', 'Priority queues', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A priority queue hands back the smallest (or largest) item first, whatever order the items were added in. A binary heap does this in logarithmic time per insert or removal, which makes it the usual tool for greedy algorithms that repeatedly take the two cheapest items.', null, 'generated'),
  ('huffman-coding', 'Huffman coding', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Huffman coding gives frequent symbols short bit codes and rare symbols long ones. It builds a binary tree by repeatedly joining the two least frequent nodes, and each symbol''s code is the path from the root to its leaf, for example 0 for left and 1 for right.', null, 'generated'),
  ('prefix-codes', 'Prefix-free codes', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'In a prefix-free code no code is the start of another, so a stream of bits can be decoded without separators: read bits until they spell a whole code, emit its symbol, and start again. Codes read from the leaves of a binary tree are prefix-free by construction.', null, 'generated'),
  ('bit-packing', 'Packing bits into bytes', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Files hold whole bytes, so single bits and codes of any length have to be packed eight to a byte. Bit n lives in byte n divided by 8, at position n modulo 8. When the last byte is not full it is padded, so the reader needs to know how many bits are real, from a stored count or the header.', null, 'generated'),
  ('file-header-format', 'Self-describing file headers', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A binary file usually starts with a header: a fixed identifier that says what kind of file it is, a version, and the numbers a reader needs before the data, such as sizes or a table. Reading checks the identifier and version first, so a wrong or newer file is rejected instead of misread, and the header says where the data starts.', null, 'generated'),
  ('byte-order', 'Byte order of numbers', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A number larger than one byte is stored as several bytes, and the order of those bytes has to be agreed. Big-endian puts the most significant byte first and is the usual order in file formats and network protocols; little-endian puts it last and is what most processors use in memory.', null, 'generated'),
  ('round-trip-testing', 'Round-trip testing', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'When one step undoes another, such as compress and decompress or save and load, feed many inputs through both and check that you get back exactly what you started with, byte for byte. Include awkward inputs, such as an empty file, a single repeated symbol and every possible byte value.', null, 'generated'),
  ('hash-functions', 'Hash functions', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A hash function turns any input into a fixed-size number. The same input always gives the same number, and a good non-cryptographic hash such as FNV-1a spreads similar inputs across the whole range. Different inputs can still collide, so code that uses hashes has to decide what a collision means.', null, 'generated'),
  ('bloom-filter', 'Bloom filters', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A Bloom filter answers "is this item in the set?" with a bit array and several hash functions. Adding an item sets the bit chosen by each hash, and a lookup that finds any of those bits clear means definitely not present. All bits set only means probably present, and the bit count and hash count are chosen from the expected number of items and the false positive rate you accept.', null, 'generated'),
  ('tcp-sockets', 'TCP sockets', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A TCP server binds to an address and port, listens, and accepts connections, each of which becomes its own socket. A connection is a two-way stream of bytes that arrives in order, and a read that returns nothing means the other side closed it.', null, 'generated'),
  ('message-framing', 'Message framing on a byte stream', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'TCP keeps no message boundaries: two sends can arrive in one read, and one send can arrive split over several. A protocol therefore marks where a message ends, with a delimiter such as a newline or a blank line, or with a length sent first, and the reader buffers bytes until a whole message is there.', null, 'generated'),
  ('concurrent-connections', 'Serving many connections at once', 'concurrency', 'foundation', null, null, 'A server that handles one connection at a time makes every other client wait, even for a slow one that sends nothing. Giving each connection its own thread, a thread from a pool, or a slot in an event loop lets clients be served side by side. Any state they share then needs protecting.', null, 'generated'),
  ('udp-datagrams', 'UDP datagrams', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'UDP sends separate messages, called datagrams, with no connection. Each read returns exactly one datagram and the address it came from, so a reply goes back to that address. Datagrams can be lost, duplicated or reordered, and nothing retries them for you.', null, 'generated'),
  ('graceful-shutdown', 'Graceful shutdown', 'concurrency', 'foundation', null, null, 'To stop a server without losing work, first stop accepting new connections, then let the work already in flight finish, then close what is left. A time limit keeps one stuck client from blocking the exit forever.', null, 'generated'),
  ('http-message-format', 'The HTTP message format', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'An HTTP/1.1 request is a request line with the method, path and version, then header lines, then an empty line, then an optional body. A response has the same shape, starting with a status line such as 200 OK. Every line ends with a carriage return and a newline, and the empty line is what ends the headers.', null, 'generated'),
  ('rest-api-design', 'Designing a REST API', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A REST API names resources with paths and acts on them with HTTP methods: POST to create, GET to read, DELETE to remove. The status code tells the client what happened, such as 201 for created, 400 for a bad request, 404 for an unknown resource and 429 for too many requests, and the body explains it.', null, 'generated'),
  ('http-redirects', 'HTTP redirects', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A redirect is a response with a 3xx status and a Location header that names where to go instead, and the browser follows it by itself. A permanent redirect such as 301 may be cached by the browser, so later visits never reach the server, while a temporary one such as 302 is asked for again each time.', null, 'generated'),
  ('short-id-generation', 'Generating short IDs', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A short ID can come from hashing the long value and keeping a few characters, from a counter, or from random characters. Hashing gives the same ID for the same input, and a counter never collides but is guessable. Short IDs from a hash or a random source can collide, so the store must check and pick another when the ID is taken.', null, 'generated'),
  ('key-value-store', 'Key-value stores', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A key-value store keeps values under unique keys and offers three basic operations: put, get and delete. It does no joins or queries across values, which makes it fast and simple to scale. A persistent store keeps the data when the process restarts, and an in-memory one loses it.', null, 'generated'),
  ('idempotency', 'Idempotent requests', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A request is idempotent when sending it twice leaves the system as sending it once does. Deleting the same item twice, or shortening the same URL twice and getting the same key back, are examples. It matters because clients retry when a reply is lost.', null, 'generated'),
  ('token-bucket', 'Token bucket rate limiting', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A token bucket holds up to a fixed number of tokens and refills at a steady rate. Each request takes one token, and a request that finds the bucket empty is rejected. The capacity sets how big a burst can be, and the refill rate sets the long-run average.', null, 'generated'),
  ('fixed-window-counter', 'Fixed window counters', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A fixed window limiter cuts time into windows of equal length, such as each whole minute, and counts requests in the current one, rejecting any over the limit. It is cheap, but a client can send a full window''s worth just before a boundary and another just after, so twice the limit in a short time.', null, 'generated'),
  ('sliding-window-log', 'Sliding window logs', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A sliding window log keeps the time of every accepted request. For each new request it drops the times older than the window and accepts only if the remaining count is under the limit. It is exact, but its memory grows with the limit for every client.', null, 'generated'),
  ('sliding-window-counter', 'Sliding window counters', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A sliding window counter keeps only two counts, for the current and the previous fixed window. It estimates the requests in the last full window by adding the current count to the previous count, weighted by how much of the previous window still overlaps. It smooths the boundary problem with almost no memory.', null, 'generated'),
  ('distributed-counters', 'Counters shared between servers', 'concurrency', 'foundation', null, null, 'When several servers enforce one limit, the count has to live in a shared store. If each server reads the count, adds one and writes it back, two servers can read the same value and one update is lost. An atomic increment in the store, which does the read and the write as one step, avoids that race.', null, 'generated'),
  ('lexicographic-order', 'Ordering text', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Sorting text compares two strings character by character and decides at the first difference, so apple comes before apricot and a shorter prefix comes first. Comparing raw byte values puts every capital letter before every small one, while a locale''s collation rules order text the way a dictionary does, so the same lines can sort differently on two machines.', null, 'generated'),
  ('sorting-algorithms', 'Sorting algorithms', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Merge sort, quicksort, heapsort and radix sort all put items in order but differ in cost. Merge sort is stable, meaning equal items keep their input order, and always takes order n log n time; quicksort is usually faster but can degrade on unlucky input; heapsort needs no extra memory; radix sort avoids comparisons altogether.', null, 'generated'),
  ('external-sorting', 'Sorting more than fits in memory', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'When the input is larger than memory, sort it in pieces that fit, write each sorted piece to a temporary file, then merge the pieces by repeatedly taking the smallest front line among them. Only one line per piece needs to be in memory during the merge.', null, 'generated'),
  ('time-space-complexity', 'How cost grows with input', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Big O notation describes how the time or memory an algorithm needs grows as the input grows, ignoring constant factors. An order n squared algorithm that is fine for a hundred lines can be hopeless for a million, so the question to ask is what happens when the input is ten times bigger.', null, 'generated'),
  ('longest-common-subsequence', 'Longest common subsequence', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A subsequence keeps items in order but may skip some, so ACE is a subsequence of ABCDE. The longest common subsequence of two sequences is the longest one that both contain, and it is what stays the same between an old and a new file. It is found with a table where each cell holds the answer for two shorter prefixes.', null, 'generated'),
  ('dynamic-programming', 'Dynamic programming', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Dynamic programming solves a problem by solving its smaller overlapping parts once each and storing the answers in a table, then building the full answer from them. It turns an exponential search into polynomial work, at the price of the memory the table needs.', null, 'generated'),
  ('edit-scripts', 'Describing changes as an edit script', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A diff describes how to turn the old version into the new one: lines only in the old version are removed, lines only in the new version are added, and the common subsequence stays. Classic diff output marks removed lines with a less-than sign and added lines with a greater-than sign, grouped by where they occur.', null, 'generated'),
  ('unit-tests-first', 'Writing tests first', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Writing a small failing test before the code makes you decide what the code should do, including edge cases such as empty input, before you decide how. Each test stays as a guard, so later changes and optimisations can be made without silently breaking what already worked.', null, 'generated'),
  ('lexer-states', 'Scanning with context states', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'The same characters mean different things in different places: two slashes start a comment in code but not inside a string. A scanner therefore tracks which context it is in, such as code, string, line comment or block comment, and each character can switch the context. A pattern search that ignores context removes too much.', null, 'generated'),
  ('meaningful-comments', 'Comments that tools read', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Some comments are instructions, not notes. Doc comments right before a declaration become documentation, build constraints decide which files compile, and directives tell a linter to skip a line. A tool that strips comments has to recognise and keep these, or it changes what the program does.', null, 'generated'),
  ('operator-precedence', 'Operator precedence and associativity', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Precedence decides which operator binds tighter, so multiplication happens before addition unless brackets say otherwise. Associativity decides the order among equal operators: subtraction and division work left to right, so 8 minus 3 minus 2 is 3, not 7.', null, 'generated'),
  ('shunting-yard', 'The shunting yard algorithm', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'The shunting yard algorithm turns an infix expression into postfix order. Numbers go straight to the output, and operators wait on a stack until an operator of lower or equal precedence, or a closing bracket, pushes them out. The result can be evaluated without any brackets.', null, 'generated'),
  ('stack-evaluation', 'Evaluating postfix with a stack', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'In postfix order an operator follows its operands, so an expression is evaluated with one stack: push each number, and for each operator pop two numbers, apply it and push the result. The second number popped is the left operand, which matters for subtraction and division. A well-formed expression leaves exactly one number.', null, 'generated'),
  ('floating-point-numbers', 'Integer and floating-point numbers', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Integers are exact, but integer division drops the remainder. Floating-point numbers hold fractions in binary, so many decimals such as 0.1 are stored approximately and small errors appear in results. A calculator has to choose how it divides and how it prints results.', null, 'generated'),
  ('interpreter-loop', 'The interpreter loop', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'An interpreter keeps a program counter that points at the current instruction and runs a loop: fetch the instruction, carry it out, move the counter on. A jump instruction sets the counter somewhere else instead. Everything else the program can touch, such as memory cells and a data pointer, is state the loop updates.', null, 'generated'),
  ('bracket-matching', 'Matching brackets with a stack', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'To pair each opening bracket with its closing one, push the position of every opening bracket on a stack and pop it when a closing bracket arrives. A pop from an empty stack, or a stack that is not empty at the end, means the brackets are unbalanced. Doing this once before running gives every jump its target.', null, 'generated'),
  ('bytecode-compilation', 'Compiling to bytecode', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Instead of interpreting source text directly, a compiler first turns it into a compact list of instructions, dropping comments and working out jump targets once. A small virtual machine then runs the list, which is faster because the slow decisions were made ahead of time.', null, 'generated'),
  ('peephole-optimization', 'Peephole optimisation', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A peephole optimiser looks at short runs of instructions and replaces them with fewer that do the same thing, such as five increments becoming one add of five, or a loop that only empties a cell becoming one clear. Each rewrite must leave the program''s output unchanged.', null, 'generated'),
  ('benchmarking', 'Measuring performance', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Measure before and after a change on the same workload and machine, run it several times, and compare like with like, such as wall time against wall time. A change that is not measured is a guess, and an optimisation that changes the output is a bug.', null, 'generated'),
  ('candidate-filtering', 'Cheap checks before expensive ones', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'When comparing many items, rule out most pairs with a cheap test first and save the expensive test for what is left. Files of different sizes cannot be duplicates, so group by size before hashing, and only compare bytes for files whose hashes match.', null, 'generated'),
  ('content-digests', 'Content digests', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A digest such as MD5 or SHA-256 reduces a file of any size to a short fingerprint, computed by streaming the file through it. Equal files always have equal digests, and different files almost never do, but MD5 collisions can be made on purpose, so a byte comparison is the final proof.', null, 'generated'),
  ('cache-invalidation', 'Knowing when a cache is stale', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A cache saves work by remembering results, but it must know when a result no longer holds. Store what the result depended on, such as a file''s size and modification time, next to it, and recompute when either has changed.', null, 'generated'),
  ('safe-destructive-actions', 'Confirming destructive actions', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A command that deletes or overwrites should show exactly what it will do, ask before doing it, and treat anything other than a clear yes as no. Keeping at least one copy of every duplicate, and logging what was removed, makes a mistake recoverable.', null, 'generated'),
  ('url-parsing', 'The parts of a URL', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A URL names a scheme, a host, an optional port and a path, as in http, eu.httpbin.org, 80 and /get. When the port is missing the scheme''s default is used, 80 for http and 443 for https, and an empty path means the root, a single slash.', null, 'generated'),
  ('dns-message-format', 'The DNS message format', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A DNS message starts with a 12-byte header: an ID that pairs a reply with its query, a flags field with bits such as query or response and recursion desired, and four counts. Then come the question, answer, authority and additional sections, with every number big-endian.', null, 'generated'),
  ('dns-name-compression', 'Encoding and compressing DNS names', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'DNS writes a name as labels, each preceded by its length, ending with a zero byte, so dns.google.com becomes 3 dns 6 google 3 com 0. To save space, a reply can replace the end of a name with a two-byte pointer whose top two bits are set, giving the offset where the rest of the name already appears.', null, 'generated'),
  ('iterative-resolution', 'Resolving a name from the root', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A resolver that does the work itself starts at a root server, which does not know the answer but names the servers for the top-level domain. It asks those, follows the next referral, and so on until an authoritative server returns the address. A referral that names a server without its address means resolving that name first.', null, 'generated'),
  ('resp-protocol', 'The Redis protocol (RESP)', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'RESP marks each value with its first byte, such as plus for a simple string, colon for an integer, dollar for a bulk string and star for an array, and ends lines with a carriage return and newline. Bulk strings and arrays give their length first, so the reader knows exactly how many bytes or items follow, even when the data has newlines.', null, 'generated'),
  ('key-expiry', 'Expiring keys', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A key with a time to live disappears after a set time. Storing the absolute moment it expires, rather than the time left, makes the check one comparison. Checking on every read catches expired keys cheaply, and an occasional background sweep removes ones nobody reads.', null, 'generated'),
  ('snapshot-persistence', 'Saving a snapshot to disk', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'An in-memory store can survive a restart by writing its whole state to a file and reading it back at startup. Writing to a temporary file and renaming it over the old one means a crash mid-write leaves the previous snapshot intact, and anything changed since the last snapshot is lost on a crash.', null, 'generated'),
  ('append-only-log', 'Append-only log storage', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'An append-only store never changes a record in place: every write, including an update, adds a new record at the end of the active file. Appending is fast and a crash can only damage the last record. The latest record for a key wins, and old ones are cleaned up later.', null, 'generated'),
  ('checksums', 'Checksums for stored records', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A checksum such as CRC-32, stored with each record, is computed again when the record is read. If the two differ the record was cut short or corrupted, for example by a crash mid-write, and it is skipped instead of being trusted.', null, 'generated'),
  ('hash-index', 'An in-memory index into log files', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'Keeping every value in memory does not scale, but keeping only where each value lives does. An in-memory hash table maps each key to the file, the position and the size of its latest value, so a read is one lookup and one disk seek.', null, 'generated'),
  ('tombstones', 'Deleting with tombstones', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'In an append-only store a delete cannot remove the old record, so it appends a tombstone, a record that says the key is gone. Reads and rebuilds treat the tombstone as the latest word on that key, and compaction drops both the tombstone and the old values.', null, 'generated'),
  ('log-compaction', 'Compacting a log', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'An append-only log keeps growing with stale values and tombstones. Compaction, which Bitcask calls merging, rewrites the closed files keeping only the latest live value of each key, can write a small hint file listing where each key now lives, and then deletes the old files.', null, 'generated'),
  ('crash-recovery', 'Recovering after a crash', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'After a crash a store rebuilds its in-memory state from what is on disk, by replaying the log from the start or by reading smaller hint files. Records that fail their checksum, usually a half-written last one, are dropped, so the store comes back with every write that fully reached the disk.', null, 'generated'),
  ('text-encoding-utf8', 'Unicode code points and UTF-8', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Unicode gives every character a number, its code point. UTF-8 stores each code point in one to four bytes, with plain ASCII in one byte, so the byte count and the character count of the same text often differ.', '{"how_it_works":"Text in a file is only bytes. To turn bytes into letters, the program that wrote them and the program that\nreads them must agree on an encoding: a rule that maps each character to bytes and back.\n\nUnicode splits this into two questions. First, which character is it? Unicode gives every character a number\ncalled its code point, written like U+0041 for the capital letter A or U+1F600 for the grinning face emoji, with\nroom for about 1.1 million of them. Second, how is that number stored? That is the job of an encoding such as\nUTF-8, UTF-16 or UTF-32.\n\nUTF-8 uses a variable number of bytes. Code points up to U+007F, which is plain ASCII, take one byte, exactly as\nin ASCII. Code points up to U+07FF take two bytes (accented Latin letters, Greek, Cyrillic, Arabic, Hebrew), up\nto U+FFFF take three (most Chinese, Japanese and Hindi text, and the euro sign), and the rest take four, which\nincludes most emoji. The first byte of a character says how many bytes it has: its leading bits are 0 for one\nbyte, or 110, 1110 or 11110 for two, three or four. Every following byte starts with the bits 10, so a reader\ndropped anywhere in a file finds the start of the next character by skipping bytes that start with 10.\n\nWhy this design? Every ASCII file is already valid UTF-8, so old files and tools kept working. No multi-byte\ncharacter contains a byte that looks like an ASCII letter, a slash, a newline or a zero, so tools that only care\nabout those bytes, such as counting lines by newline, work on UTF-8 without decoding it. And UTF-8 has no byte\norder question, because it is a sequence of single bytes.\n\nSome programs write the three bytes EF BB BF at the very start of a UTF-8 file. This byte order mark only says\n\"this is UTF-8\", and many tools do not expect it.","examples":["The word café is 4 characters but 5 bytes in UTF-8, because é (U+00E9) needs two bytes. A column limited to 4 bytes cannot hold it, and a tool that counts bytes as letters reports 5.","For years the MySQL encoding called utf8 stored at most three bytes per character, so saving an emoji in a username failed or cut the text short. The fix was a separate encoding, utf8mb4, which allows four.","A web address cannot contain é directly, so browsers send its two UTF-8 bytes in percent form: café becomes caf%C3%A9."],"misconceptions":["UTF-8 means 8 bits per character. It means the code unit is 8 bits, and a character takes one to four units.","Unicode is an encoding. Unicode is the list of characters and their numbers; UTF-8, UTF-16 and UTF-32 are ways to store those numbers.","A file''s size in bytes is its number of characters. That holds only for plain ASCII text.","A UTF-8 file must start with a byte order mark. It needs none, and the mark can break tools that read the first line or column name."],"elsewhere":["JSON exchanged between systems must be UTF-8, and so is almost every web page and API.","Databases limit text in bytes or in characters depending on the type, which matters as soon as users type other scripts or emoji.","Go and Rust strings hold UTF-8 bytes, so their length is a byte count, not a character count."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('utf16-code-units', 'UTF-16, UTF-32 and code units', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'UTF-16 stores code points in 16-bit units: one unit for most characters and a surrogate pair of two units for the rest, including most emoji. Java, JavaScript, C# and Windows use it, so their string length counts units, not characters.', '{"how_it_works":"UTF-16 stores text in units of 16 bits. A code point up to U+FFFF, the Basic Multilingual Plane that holds almost\nevery living script, fits in one unit. A code point above U+FFFF, such as U+1F600, does not fit, so it is split\nacross two units called a surrogate pair. The first comes from the high surrogate range D800 to DBFF and the\nsecond from the low surrogate range DC00 to DFFF. Those ranges are never used for real characters, so a reader\nalways recognises a pair.\n\nThis matters because Java, JavaScript, C#, Objective-C and the Windows text APIs store strings as UTF-16, and\ntheir length counts code units. A string holding one grinning face has length 2 there. Cutting such a string at\na fixed length can split a pair and leave half an emoji, which shows up as a box or a question mark. Counting or\nslicing by code point needs separate methods that each language provides.\n\nBecause a unit is two bytes, UTF-16 also needs a byte order: UTF-16LE puts the low byte first and UTF-16BE the\nhigh byte first. A file may begin with the mark FF FE or FE FF to say which.\n\nUTF-32 stores every code point in four bytes. It is simple to index, since the nth code point starts at byte 4n,\nbut it uses four times the space of ASCII text, so it is rare in files.\n\nBefore Unicode, single-byte encodings such as Latin-1 and Windows-1252 mapped 256 values to the letters of one\ngroup of languages. Reading UTF-8 bytes as Windows-1252 turns café into cafÃ©, the garbled text called mojibake.","examples":["A social app limits posts by JavaScript string length. A post of 140 emoji measures 280, so users meet a limit that seems to shrink.","A notification cuts a message at 100 units to fit the screen. If unit 100 is the first half of a surrogate pair, the phone shows a broken symbol at the end.","Excel''s Unicode Text export writes UTF-16LE with the mark FF FE, so a script expecting UTF-8 sees a zero byte after every English letter."],"misconceptions":["UTF-16 uses exactly two bytes per character. Characters above U+FFFF take four bytes, two units.","String length in Java or JavaScript counts characters. It counts UTF-16 units, so most emoji count as two.","UTF-32 gives one unit per visible character. It gives one per code point, and a visible character can be several."],"elsewhere":["JSON writes an escaped character above U+FFFF as two escapes, one per surrogate, so a parser must join them.","Windows file names are UTF-16, so tools that move files between Windows and Linux convert the names.","Any API with a length limit needs to say whether it means bytes, UTF-16 units, code points or what users see."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('grapheme-clusters', 'What users see as one character', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A grapheme cluster is what a reader sees as one character, and it can be several code points: a letter and a combining accent, an emoji and a skin tone, or emoji joined into a family. The same visible text can also be stored as different code points, which normalisation makes comparable.', '{"how_it_works":"Code points are not the characters a person sees. Unicode builds many visible characters out of several code\npoints, and calls the visible unit a grapheme cluster.\n\nCombining marks attach to the character before them: e followed by U+0301, the combining acute accent, shows as\né. Emoji use the same idea. The thumbs up U+1F44D followed by the skin tone modifier U+1F3FD shows as one thumb\nbut is two code points. A family emoji of a man, a woman and a girl is three person emoji joined by two invisible\nzero width joiners, U+200D, so it is five code points and eighteen UTF-8 bytes for one picture. A flag is two\nregional indicator letters: the Indian flag is the letters I and N.\n\nBecause of combining marks, the same visible text can be stored in more than one way. é can be the single code\npoint U+00E9, called precomposed, or e plus U+0301, called decomposed. They look identical but compare as\ndifferent strings and have different lengths and bytes. Normalisation converts text to one agreed form: NFC\nprefers precomposed characters and NFD takes them apart. Compare and store text only after normalising it the\nsame way.\n\nCounting what users see needs the segmentation rules of Unicode Annex 29, which libraries implement. Most tools,\nincluding wc with -m, count code points instead, so a family emoji counts as five characters there.","examples":["Two accounts both called José can exist side by side if one phone sent the precomposed é and another system sent e plus an accent, unless the sign-up form normalises names.","A form that limits display names to 10 characters by counting code points rejects a short name with a family emoji in it, although the user sees far fewer than 10 characters.","macOS used to store file names in decomposed form, so a file named café there could fail to match the same name typed on Linux."],"misconceptions":["One code point is one character. A visible character can be many code points.","Strings that look the same are equal. They are equal only when their code points match, which may need normalising first.","Reversing or cutting text one code point at a time is safe. It can separate an accent from its letter or break up an emoji."],"elsewhere":["Text editors move the cursor and delete by grapheme cluster, so one backspace removes a whole family emoji.","Search and databases need normalisation and collation rules so that café finds café however it was typed.","Password checks should normalise first, or the same password typed on another keyboard can fail."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('io-buffering', 'Buffered I/O and system calls', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'Every read from the operating system is a system call with a fixed cost, so reading one byte at a time is slow even though the data is the same. Reading into a buffer of some kilobytes and working from memory spreads that cost over many bytes.', '{"how_it_works":"Programs do not read files by themselves. They ask the operating system through a system call, and each call\nhas a fixed cost: switching into the kernel, checking the request, copying the data, switching back. That cost\nis small, but paid once per byte it dominates everything else.\n\nA buffer fixes this. The program asks for many bytes at once, perhaps 64 KB, then works through them in memory,\nwhere each byte costs almost nothing. One system call now serves tens of thousands of bytes. Languages offer\nbuffered readers and writers that keep such a buffer behind the scenes, so code can ask for one byte or one line\nat a time without paying a system call for each.\n\nThe operating system buffers too. Recently read parts of files stay in memory in the page cache, and the system\nreads ahead when it sees a file being read in order. That is why counting a file the second time is often much\nfaster than the first.\n\nBigger is not always better. Once the buffer holds some tens of kilobytes, the time goes into waiting for the\ndisk or copying memory, not into calls, and a bigger buffer only uses more memory. Measuring is the honest way to\nchoose a size.","examples":["Counting a 1 GB file one byte per system call makes about a billion calls and can take minutes. Reading 64 KB at a time makes about sixteen thousand calls and takes seconds.","Writing a log without a buffer slows a busy server down, so logging libraries buffer and write out the buffer on a timer or when it fills.","Running wc twice on the same large file is faster the second time, because the file now sits in the page cache."],"misconceptions":["A buffered reader holds the whole file. It holds one buffer''s worth at a time.","When code asks for one byte, the disk reads one byte. Disks and the operating system work in blocks of kilobytes.","Buffered output is written the moment the code writes it. It waits in the buffer until the buffer fills or is flushed, so a crash can lose the last part."],"elsewhere":["Sockets, database drivers and file uploads all buffer for the same reason.","Forgetting to flush a buffered writer before a program exits is a classic cause of missing last lines in output files.","Tracing tools such as strace on Linux count system calls, which exposes unbuffered code."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('malformed-input', 'Handling malformed input', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Real input breaks its format: invalid UTF-8 bytes, a truncated last record, a value out of range. A tool must decide whether to reject it, replace the bad part or skip it, and say clearly where the problem was.', '{"how_it_works":"Every format has rules, and real input breaks them: a file cut off in the middle of a write, a byte that cannot\nstart any UTF-8 character, a log line written by a buggy program. A tool cannot pretend this never happens, so it\nneeds a policy.\n\nThere are three honest choices. Reject: stop and report an error with the position of the bad input, which is\nright when wrong output would be worse than none, such as when loading configuration. Replace: put a marker in\nplace of the bad part and carry on, which is what text decoders usually do, substituting U+FFFD, the replacement\ncharacter, for each invalid sequence. Skip: drop the bad part quietly, which keeps the tool running but hides\nproblems, so it should at least be counted or logged.\n\nIn UTF-8 the invalid cases are a continuation byte with no lead byte before it, a lead byte without enough\ncontinuation bytes after it, a sequence longer than its code point needs, called overlong, and the code points\nreserved for UTF-16 surrogates. Overlong forms matter for security: an old attack wrote a slash as two bytes to\nslip past filters that only looked for the one-byte slash.\n\nWhatever the policy, write it down, apply it the same way at a chunk boundary as in the middle of a chunk, and\ntest it, ideally against a reference tool.","examples":["A log file holds a few bytes of binary data from a crashed process. A character counter that stops at the first invalid byte cannot report anything about the rest of a 20 GB file.","Browsers show U+FFFD, often drawn as a black diamond with a question mark, wherever a page''s bytes are not valid in its declared encoding.","A JSON parser must reject invalid UTF-8 in a string instead of guessing, because two systems that guess differently would read different data."],"misconceptions":["Input from files is well formed. Truncated writes, wrong encodings and binary junk happen every day.","Replacing bad bytes loses nothing important. It changes the data, so the tool should say that it happened.","Being lenient is always kinder. A lenient reader and a strict one can disagree about the same input, and attackers use that."],"elsewhere":["Parsers for JSON, HTTP, DNS and file formats all need a policy for malformed input.","Data pipelines often send bad records to a separate queue instead of failing or dropping them.","Security reviews look for places where two components treat malformed input differently."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('locale-settings', 'Locales and the environment', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A locale is a set of environment settings, such as LANG and LC_ALL, that tells programs which encoding, character classes and sorting rules to use. The same command can give different answers under two locales, so tests must set it.', '{"how_it_works":"Many Unix tools do not decide by themselves how to read text. They ask the C library, which follows the locale:\nenvironment variables such as LANG, LC_CTYPE and LC_ALL, where LC_ALL overrides the others. A locale name such as\nen_US.UTF-8 or C.UTF-8 says which language conventions to follow and which encoding to decode with.\n\nThe plain C locale, also called POSIX, treats every byte as one character and only ASCII letters as letters. In\nthat locale wc -m counts bytes, so it gives the same number as wc -c, and é counts as two characters. In a UTF-8\nlocale wc -m decodes UTF-8 and counts code points. The locale also decides which characters are whitespace for\nwc -w, which characters grep calls letters, and how sort orders lines.\n\nSo a result can change without any change to the file or the program: another terminal, a scheduled job with an\nalmost empty environment, a container image built without locale data, or a CI runner. Git Bash on Windows, for\nexample, sets a UTF-8 locale in its terminal window but not always for scripts, and its wc counts an emoji above\nU+FFFF as two characters even in UTF-8.\n\nTo get reproducible results, set the locale explicitly whenever a test or script runs a tool, and record which\nlocale an expected value was taken with.","examples":["The challenge''s test file gives 339292 for wc -m in a UTF-8 locale and 342190, its byte count, in the C locale, on the same machine.","A nightly job that sorts customer names produces a different order from the same command typed in a terminal, because the scheduler runs it with the C locale.","A container image without locale data falls back to C, so a tool that handled accented names on a laptop treats them as bytes in production."],"misconceptions":["A program behaves the same wherever it runs. Its environment, including the locale, is part of its input.","The locale only changes language and date formats. It also decides decoding, character classes and sort order.","A UTF-8 file is always read as UTF-8. The tool uses the locale''s encoding, whatever the file holds."],"elsewhere":["sort, grep, awk and many language runtimes read the locale.","Databases have a collation setting that plays the same role for sorting and comparing text.","Number formats differ by locale, such as a comma or a point before decimals, which breaks parsing numbers written on another machine."]}'::jsonb, 'generated')
